NNAAMMEE
       dpkg-query - a tool to query the dpkg database

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ddppkkgg--qquueerryy [_o_p_t_i_o_n...] _c_o_m_m_a_n_d

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ddppkkgg--qquueerryy is a tool to show information about packages listed in the
       ddppkkgg database.

CCOOMMMMAANNDDSS
       --ll, ----lliisstt [_p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e_-_p_a_t_t_e_r_n...]
           List all known packages matching one or more patterns, regardless
           of their status, which includes any real or virtual package
           referenced in any dependency relationship field (such as BBrreeaakkss,
           EEnnhhaanncceess, etc.).  If no _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e_-_p_a_t_t_e_r_n is given, list all
           packages in _/_v_a_r_/_l_i_b_/_d_p_k_g_/_s_t_a_t_u_s, excluding the ones marked as not-
           installed (i.e.  those which have been previously purged).  Normal
           shell wildcard characters are allowed in _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e_-_p_a_t_t_e_r_n.
           Please note you will probably have to quote _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e_-_p_a_t_t_e_r_n to
           prevent the shell from performing filename expansion.  For example
           this will list all package names starting with “libc6”:

            dpkg-query -l 'libc6*'

           The first three columns of the output show the desired action, the
           package status, and errors, in that order.

           Desired action:

           u = Unknown
           i = Install
           h = Hold
           r = Remove
           p = Purge

           Package status:

           n = Not-installed
           c = Config-files
           H = Half-installed
           U = Unpacked
           F = Half-configured
           W = Triggers-awaiting
           t = Triggers-pending
           i = Installed

           Error flags:

           <empty> = (none)
           R = Reinst-required

           An uppercase status or error letter indicates the package is likely
           to cause severe problems. Please refer to ddppkkgg(1) for information
           about the above states and flags.

           The output format of this option is not configurable, but varies
           automatically to fit the terminal width. It is intended for human
           readers, and is not easily machine-readable. See --WW (----sshhooww) and
           ----sshhoowwffoorrmmaatt for a way to configure the output format.

       --WW, ----sshhooww [_p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e_-_p_a_t_t_e_r_n...]
           Just like the ----lliisstt option this will list all packages matching
           the given patterns. However the output can be customized using the
           ----sshhoowwffoorrmmaatt option.

           The default output format gives one line per matching package, each
           line consisting of the package name and its installed version,
           separated by a tab.  The package name will be architecture
           qualified for packages with a MMuullttii--AArrcchh field with the value ssaammee
           or with a foreign architecture, which is an architecture that is
           neither the native one nor aallll.

       --ss, ----ssttaattuuss [_p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e...]
           Report status of specified packages. This just displays the entry
           in the installed package status database.  If no _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e is
           specified it will display all package entries in the status
           database (since dpkg 1.19.1).  When multiple _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e entries
           are listed, the requested status entries are separated by an empty
           line, with the same order as specified on the argument list.

       --LL, ----lliissttffiilleess _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e...
           List files installed to your system from _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e. When
           multiple _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_es are listed, the requested lists of files are
           separated by an empty line, with the same order as specified on the
           argument list.

           Each file diversion is printed on its own line after its diverted
           file, prefixed with one of the following localized strings:


             locally diverted to: _d_i_v_e_r_t_e_d_-_t_o
             package diverts others to: _d_i_v_e_r_t_e_d_-_t_o
             diverted by _p_k_g to: _d_i_v_e_r_t_e_d_-_t_o

           Hint: When machine parsing the output, it is customary to set the
           locale to CC..UUTTFF--88 to get reproducible results.

           This command will not list extra files created by maintainer
           scripts, nor will it list alternatives.

       ----ccoonnttrrooll--lliisstt _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e
           List control files installed to your system from _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e
           (since dpkg 1.16.5).  These can be used as input arguments to
           ----ccoonnttrrooll--sshhooww.

       ----ccoonnttrrooll--sshhooww _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e _c_o_n_t_r_o_l_-_f_i_l_e
           Print the _c_o_n_t_r_o_l_-_f_i_l_e installed to your system from _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e
           to the standard output (since dpkg 1.16.5).

       --cc, ----ccoonnttrrooll--ppaatthh _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e [_c_o_n_t_r_o_l_-_f_i_l_e]
           List paths for control files installed to your system from _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-
           _n_a_m_e (since dpkg 1.15.4).  If _c_o_n_t_r_o_l_-_f_i_l_e is specified then only
           list the path for that control file if it is present.

           WWaarrnniinngg: this command is deprecated as it gives direct access to
           the internal dpkg database, please switch to use ----ccoonnttrrooll--lliisstt and
           ----ccoonnttrrooll--sshhooww instead for all cases where those commands might
           give the same end result. Although, as long as there is still at
           least one case where this command is needed (i.e. when having to
           remove a damaging postrm maintainer script), and while there is no
           good solution for that, this command will not get removed.

       --SS, ----sseeaarrcchh _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e_-_s_e_a_r_c_h_-_p_a_t_t_e_r_n...
           Search for packages that own files corresponding to the given
           patterns.  Standard shell wildcard characters can be used in the
           pattern, where asterisk (**) and question mark (??) will match a
           slash, and backslash (\\) will be used as an escape character.

           If the first character in the _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e_-_s_e_a_r_c_h_-_p_a_t_t_e_r_n is none of
           ‘**[[??//’ then it will be considered a substring match and will be
           implicitly surrounded by ‘**’ (as in **_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e_-_s_e_a_r_c_h_-_p_a_t_t_e_r_n**).  If
           the subsequent string contains any of ‘**[[??\\’, then it will handled
           like a glob pattern, otherwise any trailing ‘//’ or ‘//..’ will be
           removed and a literal path lookup will be performed.

           This command will not list extra files created by maintainer
           scripts, nor will it list alternatives.

           The output format consists of one line per matching pattern, with a
           list of packages owning the pathname separated by a comma (U+002C
           ‘,,’) and a space (U+0020 ‘ ’), followed by a colon (U+003A ‘::’) and
           a space, followed by the pathname. As in:

             pkgname1, pkgname2: pathname1
             pkgname3: pathname2

           File diversions are printed with the following localized strings:


             diversion by _p_k_g_n_a_m_e from: _d_i_v_e_r_t_e_d_-_f_r_o_m
             diversion by _p_k_g_n_a_m_e to: _d_i_v_e_r_t_e_d_-_t_o

           or for local diversions:


             local diversion from: _d_i_v_e_r_t_e_d_-_f_r_o_m
             local diversion to: _d_i_v_e_r_t_e_d_-_t_o

           Hint: When machine parsing the output, it is customary to set the
           locale to CC..UUTTFF--88 to get reproducible results.

       --pp, ----pprriinntt--aavvaaiill [_p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e...]
           Display details about packages, as found in
           _/_v_a_r_/_l_i_b_/_d_p_k_g_/_a_v_a_i_l_a_b_l_e.  If no _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e is specified, it will
           display all package entries in the _a_v_a_i_l_a_b_l_e database (since dpkg
           1.19.1).  When multiple _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e are listed, the requested
           _a_v_a_i_l_a_b_l_e entries are separated by an empty line, with the same
           order as specified on the argument list.

           Users of APT-based frontends should use aapptt sshhooww _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_-_n_a_m_e
           instead as the _a_v_a_i_l_a_b_l_e file is only kept up-to-date when using
           ddsseelleecctt.

       --??, ----hheellpp
           Show the usage message and exit.

       ----vveerrssiioonn
           Show the version and exit.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       ----aaddmmiinnddiirr==_d_i_r
           Change the location of the ddppkkgg database. The default location is
           _/_v_a_r_/_l_i_b_/_d_p_k_g.

       ----rroooott==_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y
           Set the root directory to _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y, which sets the administrative
           directory to «_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y/var/lib/dpkg» (since dpkg 1.21.0).

       ----llooaadd--aavvaaiill
           Also load the available file when using the ----sshhooww and ----lliisstt
           commands, which now default to only querying the status file (since
           dpkg 1.16.2).

       ----nnoo--ppaaggeerr
           Disables the use of any pager when showing information (since dpkg
           1.19.2).

       --ff, ----sshhoowwffoorrmmaatt==_f_o_r_m_a_t
           This option is used to specify the format of the output ----sshhooww will
           produce (short option since dpkg 1.13.1).  The format is a string
           that will be output for each package listed.

           In the format string, “\\” introduces escapes:

           \\nn newline
           \\rr carriage return
           \\tt tab

           “\\” before any other character suppresses any special meaning of
           the following character, which is useful for “\\” and “$$”.

           Package information can be included by inserting variable
           references to package fields using the syntax “$${{_f_i_e_l_d[;;_w_i_d_t_h]}}”.
           Fields are printed right-aligned unless the width is negative in
           which case left alignment will be used. The following _f_i_e_l_ds are
           recognized but they are not necessarily available in the status
           file (only internal fields or fields stored in the binary package
           end up in it):

           AArrcchhiitteeccttuurree
           BBuuggss
           CCoonnffffiilleess (internal)
           CCoonnffiigg--VVeerrssiioonn (internal)
           CCoonnfflliiccttss
           BBrreeaakkss
           DDeeppeennddss
           DDeessccrriippttiioonn
           EEnnhhaanncceess
           PPrrootteecctteedd
           EEsssseennttiiaall
           FFiilleennaammee (internal, front-end related)
           HHoommeeppaaggee
           IInnssttaalllleedd--SSiizzee
           MMDD55ssuumm (internal, front-end related)
           MMSSDDOOSS--FFiilleennaammee (internal, front-end related)
           MMaaiinnttaaiinneerr
           OOrriiggiinn
           PPaacckkaaggee
           PPrree--DDeeppeennddss
           PPrriioorriittyy
           PPrroovviiddeess
           RReeccoommmmeennddss
           RReeppllaacceess
           RReevviissiioonn (obsolete)
           SSeeccttiioonn
           SSiizzee (internal, front-end related)
           SSoouurrccee
           SSttaattuuss (internal)
           SSuuggggeessttss
           TTaagg (usually not in .deb but in repository Packages files)
           TTrriiggggeerrss--AAwwaaiitteedd (internal)
           TTrriiggggeerrss--PPeennddiinngg (internal)
           VVeerrssiioonn

           The following are virtual fields, generated by ddppkkgg--qquueerryy from
           values from other fields (note that these do not use valid names
           for fields in control files):

           bbiinnaarryy::PPaacckkaaggee
               It contains the binary package name with a possible
               architecture qualifier like “libc6:amd64” (since dpkg 1.16.2).
               An architecture qualifier will be present to make the package
               name unambiguous, for packages with a MMuullttii--AArrcchh field with the
               value ssaammee or with a foreign architecture, which is an
               architecture that is neither the native one nor aallll.

           bbiinnaarryy::SSyynnooppssiiss
               It contains the package short description (since dpkg 1.19.1).

           bbiinnaarryy::SSuummmmaarryy
               This is an alias for bbiinnaarryy::SSyynnooppssiiss (since dpkg 1.16.2).

           ddbb::SSttaattuuss--AAbbbbrreevv
               It contains the abbreviated package status (as three
               characters), such as “ii ” or “iHR” (since dpkg 1.16.2).  See
               the ----lliisstt command description for more details.

           ddbb::SSttaattuuss--WWaanntt
               It contains the package wanted status, part of the Status field
               (since dpkg 1.17.11).

           ddbb::SSttaattuuss--SSttaattuuss
               It contains the package status word, part of the Status field
               (since dpkg 1.17.11).

           ddbb::SSttaattuuss--EEffllaagg
               It contains the package status error flag, part of the Status
               field (since dpkg 1.17.11).

           ddbb--ffssyyss::FFiilleess
               It contains the list of the package filesystem entries
               separated by newlines (since dpkg 1.19.3).

           ddbb--ffssyyss::LLaasstt--MMooddiiffiieedd
               It contains the timestamp in seconds of the last time the
               package filesystem entries were modified (since dpkg 1.19.3).

           ssoouurrccee::PPaacckkaaggee
               It contains the source package name for this binary package
               (since dpkg 1.16.2).

           ssoouurrccee::VVeerrssiioonn
               It contains the source package version for this binary package
               (since dpkg 1.16.2)

           ssoouurrccee::UUppssttrreeaamm--VVeerrssiioonn
               It contains the source package upstream version for this binary
               package (since dpkg 1.18.16)

           The default format string is “$${{bbiinnaarryy::PPaacckkaaggee}}\\tt$${{VVeerrssiioonn}}\\nn”.
           Actually, all other fields found in the status file (i.e. user
           defined fields) can be requested, too. They will be printed as-is,
           though, no conversion nor error checking is done on them. To get
           the name of the ddppkkgg maintainer and the installed version, you
           could run:

            dpkg-query -f='${binary:Package} ${Version}\t${Maintainer}\n' \
             -W dpkg

